<h2>Start with disclosure, not argument</h2>
<p>Since <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> (Criminal Appeal No. 730 of 2020, decided 4 November 2020), quantum debates begin with sworn affidavits of assets, income and liabilities from both parties, in the formats the judgment prescribes. The affidavit regime exists because maintenance litigation was drowning in strategic vagueness — husbands understating income, wives overstating needs, and courts guessing between them. The affidavits convert the contest into a documents case: salary slips, bank statements, tax returns, business receipts, household expenses. A party who suppresses or fudges does so at real cost, because the court may draw inferences against the author of an incomplete affidavit.</p>

<h2>The factors, organised</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Marital standard of living.</strong> Maintenance aims to let the claimant live with reasonable comfort judged by the life the marriage established — neither a pauper&rsquo;s allowance nor a windfall.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Needs and independent income.</strong> A claimant&rsquo;s own earnings reduce, but do not automatically eliminate, the claim; capacity to earn is weighed differently from actual earnings.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Payer&rsquo;s real capacity.</strong> Net income after tax and legitimate obligations — including support of dependent parents and children of a subsequent marriage — is the base on which any fraction operates.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Children&rsquo;s costs.</strong> School fees, tuition, medical and extracurricular expenses are typically provided for separately from the spouse&rsquo;s own maintenance.</div>
</div>

<h2>The 25% benchmark, used honestly</h2>
<p>The one-fourth figure traces to <em>Dr. Kulbhushan Kumar v. Raj Kumari</em>, (1970) 3 SCC 129, where 25% of the husband&rsquo;s net salary was held just and proper — a holding the Supreme Court recalled with approval in <em>Kalyan Dey Chowdhury</em> (Civil Appeal No. 5369 of 2017, decided 19 April 2017). Three cautions keep the benchmark honest. First, it is a cross-check, not a computation: the court reasons from the factors to a figure and then asks whether the result is defensible against the payer&rsquo;s net means. Second, it addresses the spouse&rsquo;s maintenance against a salaried income; business incomes, multiple dependants and child-support components all complicate the fraction. Third, the benchmark bends to facts: in <em>Kalyan Dey</em> itself the Court fixed Rs 20,000 against a net salary of about Rs 95,527 — beneath one-fourth — expressly because the husband had remarried and had a child from the second marriage. The governing phrases in that judgment are the real test: maintenance must be <em>befitting the status of the parties and the capacity of the spouse to pay</em>, and is <em>always dependent on the factual situation of the case</em>.</p>

<h2>Arguing quantum like a professional</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Build the budget: a claimant&rsquo;s side should file a line-item monthly budget tied to receipts, not a round number. Specificity reads as honesty.</p>
  <p>Reconstruct the payer&rsquo;s lifestyle where income is understated: rent actually paid, vehicles, travel, club and card statements. Courts routinely impute income from lifestyle.</p>
  <p>For payers: document every legitimate obligation — EMIs predating the dispute, dependent parents, a second family — because these move the base figure, as Kalyan Dey shows.</p>
  <p>Address the date-of-application rule from the first hearing: arrears accumulate from filing, and a realistic interim figure beats a high figure later slashed with an impossible arrears bill.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer sets out general principles for information. Maintenance in any case depends on its own record, and figures from reported decisions are illustrations, not entitlements.</div>
